[Music]  back now at 7 30 on cbs mornings and we  bring you a six-month cbs news  investigation that raises new questions  about how effectively state medical  boards can hold bad doctors accountable  and thereby protect patients this  morning we hear from the for the first  time from an insider who sits on  california's medical board which is  responsible for licensing and  disciplining more than 150 000 doctors  in part one of our two-part series chief  investigative correspondent jim axelrod  looks at the case of a doctor who was  allowed to continue practicing despite  repeated misconduct that the medical  board knew about jim good morning good  morning tony in california a board of 15  people makes these decisions about when  a doctor should lose his or her license  to practice now eight of the 15 are  other physicians  our investigation raised questions about  who that board is protecting the public  or the doctors  i think i was absolutely botched  there's just no other description for it  brandi williams is describing a 2016  surgery she thought would help her get  her confidence back after losing almost  30 pounds williams decided to reward her  hard work with a procedure known as an  abdominoplasty or tummy tuck the  doctor's office is supposed to be a  place of healing and that's not what  this place was for me my name is jason  hellowell to do the surgery brandi chose  dr jason heliwell an ob gyn who also  conducts cosmetic procedures he  practices in this building behind me  right here in bakersfield but brandi  says she only chose him after first  conducting her own research into his  record  i took the business card from his office  which has his medical license number on  it i went home  found out if there were any complaints  against him it was blank  there was no complaints against him we  discovered dr hellerwell had been  disciplined for negligence five years  earlier i was in pain for a year and a  half according to a malpractice lawsuit  williams settled with dr hellowell her  surgery left her with a painful hematoma  that required surgical repair  williams says that pain drove her to  file a complaint about dr hellewell to  the california medical board it's their  job to protect us from doctors who  make mistakes i'm not saying he's not  human we all make mistakes but he made a  whopping mistake when it came to me  what could have been helpful to williams  was knowing another patient had already  reported dr hellowell it's not like  i went to a car dealership and went to  buy a car this is my life six weeks  after delivering her child emily yost  says dr hellewell told her she needed  umbilical hernia surgery and he could do  the procedure in his office  even though emily says it had been  scheduled for the hospital knowing what  i know now i would have switched doctors  immediately okay this is my online  complaint summary yoast sent this  complaint to the california medical  board explaining she had to have an  additional surgery to fix the surgery  that dr hellewell performed  i never got an email that said  hey we'll look into it we're sorry that  happened nothing  nothing happened  but cbs news has learned that while the  medical board dismissed brandie williams  complaint  and never even bothered to contact emily  yost the california medical board had  been investigating dr hellewell the  entire time this 54-page ruling details  a laundry list of misconduct by dr  hellewell dating back to 2013 including  a sexual relationship with a patient  administering toxic dosage of lidocaine  and gross negligence in the treatment of  four patients  investigators concluded hellowell's care  of one patient showed a fundamental lack  of knowledge but in 2018 the california  medical board did not revoke dr  hellewell's medical license  instead they placed him on 42 months  probation with limits on his practice  since the medical board's ruling dr  hellewell has been arrested and charged  with 32 felony counts for a fraudulent  medical billing scheme  his medical license in california  remains active  what does a doctor have to do in  california to lose his or her license  that is a great question  and the answer is i don't know  tj watkins currently sits on the  california medical board he's speaking  out for the first time  is the california medical board doing  its job  absolutely no  cbs news found that since 2015 the  medical board received more than 50 000  complaints against doctors yet they  revoked just 201 licenses  less than one-half of one percent  do you think the way  the medical board functions actually  ends up costing patient lives  there's no question that it costs  patients lives no question no question  dr hellewell declined an interview when  we reached out with questions we never  heard back now as for the medical board  it said the law requires it to  rehabilitate doctors when possible  although the board does tell us it is  currently looking into the criminal  allegations against dr hellowell  tomorrow in part two of our  investigation you will hear more from  our exclusive interview with california  board member t.j watkins who says the  board does allow dangerous doctors to  keep practicing  i think that i'm really looking forward  to hearing what mr watkins has to say i  think it's extraordinary that he's still  sitting on the board he's criticizing  the people that he's working with that's  a big deal and and in very clear and  unambiguous terms he will continue that  tomorrow morning and speaking of  ambiguity i mean there should be  standards for practice and when you  violate those standards your license  should be revoked but no one seems to  know where the line is well a lot of  this comes back to the makeup of the  board now remember you have a 15-member  board majority rules and you've got a  majority of documents so everything sort  of funnels back to that controlling  premise when it comes to how does this  board function how are the women doing  brandy and emily i mean i think you sit  with them and there's this overarching  question of wait a minute  if if the board's not going to protect  us who does exactly so in addition to  all the the suffering and the trauma  there's this question of who's going to  take care of us yeah looking forward to  part two thanks no doubt about

